## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to reason about streaming similarity with limited memory, a practical beginner-level challenge that still touches core complexity trade-offs.

## Key Concepts
- MinHash sketches for Jaccard similarity
- Batch processing costs vs per-element costs
- Trade-offs: d (accuracy) vs memory, impact of collisions
- Exact vs approximate counting implications

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo: initialize sketches for A and B
class MinHash {
  constructor(d, maxHash) { this.d = d; this.sig = Array(d).fill(Infinity); this.maxHash = maxHash; }
  add(x) { for (let i=0; i<this.d; i++) { const h = hash_i(x, i) % this.maxHash; if (h < this.sig[i]) this.sig[i] = h; } }
  resembles(other) { let matches = 0; for (let i=0; i<this.d; i++) if (this.sig[i] === other.sig[i]) matches++; return matches / this.d; }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you choose d given target accuracy ε?
- How handle skewed data or duplicates in batches?
- How would you extend to weighted or multi-set similarity?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design simple online statistics with fixed small memory. Mode is a practical KPI for signal quality in routing decisions on ride-hailing platforms. It requires maintaining counts and batch updates.

## Key Concepts

- Online counters on a small alphabet
- Tie-breaking and stability
- Time/memory trade-offs in streaming

## Code Example

```javascript
function OnlineMode() {
  const freq = [0,0,0,0,0,0]; // 1..5
  let mode = 1, modeCount = 0;
  return {
    update(batch){
      for (const r of batch){
        freq[r]++;
        if (freq[r] > modeCount || (freq[r] === modeCount && r < mode)){
          mode = r; modeCount = freq[r];
        }
      }
      return {mode, modeCount};
    }
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt for a larger rating scale (e.g., 1–1000)?
- How would you support sliding-window mode across the last K batches?

